CAT = "nzfaTarget"; SEG = "TGT"
Q = [
("In about what share of hunting shooting incidents are shooter and victim in the same party?",
 "About 80%", ["About 20%", "About 50%", "About 95%"],
 "Research quoted in the Firearms Safety Code found that in 80% of incidents the shooter and victim were in the same hunting group, typically after the group split up and lost contact."),
("In most misidentification incidents, how far apart were the shooter and the victim?",
 "Under 75 metres", ["Over 300 metres", "About 150 metres", "Over a kilometre"],
 "In 92% of incidents the range between shooter and victim was less than 75 metres. Close range and poor visibility do not make identification easier."),
("Incidents are more common in heavy bush than open country mainly because of:",
 "Reduced visibility", ["Steeper ground", "Larger hunting parties", "Faster game animals"],
 "Heavy bush cuts visibility and leads to reactive shots, so misidentification incidents are more common there than in open high country."),
("What is \"buck fever\" or \"stag fever\"?",
 "Excitement overriding reason", ["A disease spread by wild deer", "Tiredness after a long stalk", "Fear of shooting an animal"],
 "Buck fever is the stress and excitement of sighting game, with a raised heart rate, that leads to poor awareness and rash decisions."),
("Under stress, tunnel vision means:",
 "Your field of view narrows", ["You see better in low light", "You cannot judge distance", "You see double images"],
 "With tunnel vision the area in front looks clear while everything around it blurs or darkens, so people at the side of your firing zone can go unseen."),
("Auditory exclusion under stress means you may:",
 "Not hear others warning you", ["Hear echoes off the hillsides", "Mistake sounds for deer", "Lose hearing from the shot"],
 "Auditory exclusion is a temporary loss of hearing under high stress, often with tunnel vision. You may not hear a companion calling out to you."),
("Target fixation can cause you to:",
 "Miss people in your firing zone", ["Shoot too high over the target", "Lose your sight picture", "Flinch before the shot"],
 "Target fixation is focusing so hard on the animal that you lose awareness of your surroundings, including people, vehicles or buildings in your firing zone."),
("What should you use to scan an area and identify a possible target?",
 "Binoculars", ["Your rifle scope", "A rifle-mounted torch", "Your thermal rifle sight"],
 "Use binoculars or a spotting scope. Scanning with any sight mounted on the firearm means pointing the muzzle at everything you look at."),
("Why is scanning the bush through your rifle scope unsafe?",
 "It points the rifle at everything", ["It fogs the lenses in cold air", "It drains the scope's battery", "It gives too wide a field of view"],
 "Using the rifle's optic to look around breaks Rule 2: the muzzle points at whatever you are looking at, which could be another hunter."),
("Shooting with both eyes open helps to:",
 "Reduce tunnel vision", ["Judge the wind direction", "Steady the trigger press", "Increase the magnification"],
 "The code recommends aiming with both eyes open where you can, because it helps reduce tunnel vision while you are on the target."),
("Through the scrub you see antlers and brown hide. Is that enough to shoot?",
 "No, see the whole animal", ["Yes, if the antlers are clear", "Yes, if it is moving away", "Yes, if no one answers a call"],
 "Skin, shape or antlers alone are not positive identification. Look at the whole animal: type, sex, age, size and antler points, and make sure it is not a hunter carrying a deer."),
("Why should you never fire at a sound alone?",
 "Hunters may imitate deer calls", ["Sound travels too slowly", "Deer seldom make any noise", "Echoes hide the animal's true range"],
 "Hunters imitate animal calls, such as a stag roaring, so a sound may be a person. Never fire at sound, shape, colour or movement alone."),
("Which of these is a safe basis for firing at an animal?",
 "You identified it beyond all doubt", ["Movement where deer often feed", "A colour matching deer hide", "A shape that looked just like a stag"],
 "Rule 4: identify your target beyond all doubt. Movement, colour and shape alone have all led to people being shot."),
("Why might your brain \"see\" a deer that is not there?",
 "It fills gaps with what it expects", ["Eyes adjust slowly to dim bush light", "Deer and people move alike", "Scopes distort colours"],
 "When there is not enough information, especially in poor light or with the object partly hidden, the brain may show you what you expect to see rather than what is there."),
("Which two hi-vis colours are most used in New Zealand hunting gear?",
 "Blaze orange and blue", ["Red and white", "Yellow and green", "Camouflage green and brown"],
 "Blaze orange and blue are the two most popular hi-vis colours. The aim is to be as obvious as possible to other hunters, the opposite of camouflage."),
("When should hi-vis hunting gear be replaced?",
 "When its colour fades", ["After every hunting season", "Once it gets muddy", "Only if it is torn"],
 "Hi-vis fades with use and washing, and faded blaze orange can be mistaken for deer hide, so check it before each trip and replace it as needed."),
("Hunting in thick bush, hi-vis clothing should cover:",
 "A larger area of your body", ["Only your hat or cap", "Only your back and shoulders", "Only the top of your pack"],
 "Thick bush means reduced visibility and faster encounters, so wear hi-vis over a larger area. In open country less coverage is needed."),
("Carrying a deer carcass out through the bush, it is wise to:",
 "Put hi-vis over the carcass", ["Carry it out after dark", "Cover it with green scrim netting", "Hold the antlers up high"],
 "A hunter carrying an animal can be mistaken for game. The code suggests placing a hi-vis item over the carcass."),
("You are supervising an unlicensed shooter. Who must identify the target?",
 "Both of you", ["Only the shooter", "Only the supervisor", "Whoever saw it first"],
 "When supervising an unlicensed shooter, the supervisor and the shooter must both positively identify the target beyond all doubt."),
("Why is target identification harder at night?",
 "Size and colour look different", ["Animals stand still at night", "Bullets drop more in cold air", "Scopes cannot focus in the dark"],
 "At night your perception of size and colour is affected, and lights and night vision devices may not clearly show the target."),
("Reflections in a spotlight beam have been mistaken for:",
 "Animal eyes", ["Stars on water", "Car headlights", "Wet fence wire"],
 "Reflections from headlamps and other objects have been mistaken for animal eyes, and people have been seriously injured or killed as a result."),
("Pressure to bring home meat or a trophy can lead hunters to:",
 "React too fast to movement", ["Shoot more slowly than usual", "Use binoculars more often", "Hunt closer to their vehicle"],
 "Pressure, from yourself or others, has led hunters to react too quickly to movement, shape and sound instead of working through target identification."),
("You feel very tired the night before a planned hunt. The code advises:",
 "Postponing the trip", ["Hunting only from a stand", "Taking caffeine and going", "Hunting alone to go faster"],
 "Fatigue leads to poor decisions about shooting and routes. If you feel its effects before a trip, postpone it until you are in a better frame of mind."),
("Before taking a shot, which question does the code tell you to ask?",
 "Could this be another hunter?", ["Is it the biggest I have seen?", "Will it be easy to carry out?", "Is the light good for a photo?"],
 "The safer process asks: Is this a person? Could this be another hunter? Has my partner strayed into this area? Is this definitely the animal I am hunting?"),
("When you aim at an animal you have identified, the shot should go to its:",
 "Vital zone", ["Head only", "Hindquarters", "Most visible part"],
 "Aim at the vital zone for a quick, humane kill, as the code's deer hunting example shows. A shot at whatever part is visible risks wounding."),
("A deer is half hidden behind a log. What should you do?",
 "Wait until you see it all", ["Shoot where its body must be", "Shoot the visible antlers", "Fire to make it move"],
 "You must see and analyse the whole animal before firing. If you cannot identify it beyond all doubt, do not shoot."),
("Does wearing a small hi-vis cap make sure other hunters will see you?",
 "No, it may be hidden from view", ["Yes, as long as it is blaze orange", "Yes, in open country", "Yes, if it is brand new"],
 "A small hi-vis item is no guarantee: tree trunks, foliage, terrain or light can hide it. Think about how you appear from every direction."),
]
